# Policy the architecture leaves open on purpose — these come from bench
# measurement, not from a number picked in advance. See
# docs/firmware_architecture.md "Open items".
#
# Kept out of app/Kconfig so the test suites can source the same symbols the
# application builds with: a suite that hardcoded 600 ms would keep passing
# after someone changed the window.

config TK_BUTTON_DEBOUNCE_MS
    int "Category and Next debounce (ms)"
    default 30
    help
      One physical press must produce exactly one event, on either button.
      Press duration has no meaning: a long press means the same as a short
      one. The debounce itself is done by the in-tree gpio-keys driver, so this
      must match `debounce-interval-ms` in the board overlay — devicetree is
      processed before Kconfig and cannot read it.

config TK_CORPUS_LANGUAGE
    string "Language of the corpus compiled into the image"
    default "en"
    help
      The factory-preloaded question set, so a device whose Wi-Fi is never
      configured still works. Must match a bundle built by `just fw-fixtures`.

config TK_PANEL_MARGIN_PX
    int "Blank border around the text (pixels)"
    default 10
    help
      Without this the text runs to the physical edge of the glass, which reads
      as a fault rather than as a margin. Ten pixels is one character cell.

config TK_PANEL_COLUMNS
    int "Characters per line the panel fits"
    default 23
    help
      (250 - 2 * margin) / 10, the cell width of cfb_font_1016. At 23 columns
      the longest shipped question needs five lines. panel.cpp BUILD_ASSERTs
      that this agrees with the margin and the display width, so a wrong value
      fails the build rather than the glass.

config TK_PANEL_LINES
    int "Lines of text the panel fits"
    default 6
    help
      (122 - 2 * margin) / 16, the cell height of cfb_font_1016. Six lines
      against the five the corpus needs.

config TK_PANEL_AUTOSIZE
    bool "Pick the largest font each question fits in"
    default y
    help
      CFB ships three sizes — 10x16, 15x24 and 20x32. Choosing the biggest one
      a question fits in uses the whole panel instead of leaving a band of
      white under every short question. Against the shipped corpus that is
      about a third of questions at 15x24 and a handful at 20x32; the rest fall
      back to 10x16, the size the layout suite checks every question against.

      Turn off to render everything at 10x16.

config TK_PANEL_MAX_LEADING_PCT
    int "Most extra space between lines, as a percentage of the font height"
    default 50
    range 0 100
    help
      A question rarely fills its font's last line, and stacking the lines
      solid leaves that slack as a band of white above and below. Sharing it
      between the lines instead uses the panel: across the shipped corpus that
      lifts vertical fill from 66% to 82% on average.

      Capped, because past a point the lines stop reading as one question and
      start looking like separate sentences. Set to 0 to set the text solid,
      which is worth comparing on real hardware — spreading lines apart is not
      the same as setting them larger, and only a font between 15x24 and 20x32
      would do that.

config TK_PANEL_BOLD
    bool "Thicken glyphs by overdrawing"
    default y
    help
      cfb_font_1016 is a one-pixel-stroke terminal font, and on e-paper at
      arm's length it reads thin. Drawing each glyph twice, one pixel apart,
      doubles the stroke without a second font in flash. Turn off to compare.

config TK_DEBUG_CHARSET
    bool "Show character-set test pages instead of questions"
    default n
    help
      Replaces the drawn question with a series of test pages — ASCII, then one
      per language, then every diacritic the renderer composes. Next steps
      through them and each page is also logged, so `just fw-monitor` says what
      the panel should be showing. For checking glyphs and accents on real
      hardware; never enabled in a shipped build.

config TK_DEBUG_SOAK
    bool "Press Next automatically, forever"
    default n
    help
      Turns the device into its own operator: every completed refresh schedules
      the next press, so the panel redraws unattended for as long as it is
      powered. It exists to measure TK_FULL_REFRESH_INTERVAL, which needs a
      long unbroken chain of partial refreshes to watch ghosting accumulate in.

      Set by app/soak.conf, which also suppresses full refreshes for the length
      of the run. Never enabled in a shipped build.

config TK_DEBUG_SOAK_INTERVAL_MS
    int "Wait between a finished refresh and the next automatic press (ms)"
    default 5000
    depends on TK_DEBUG_SOAK
    help
      Long enough to read the question, short enough that forty refreshes take
      four minutes. Measured from the end of one refresh rather than on a fixed
      period, so a slow panel spaces the run out instead of having presses
      dropped.

config TK_DEBUG_SOAK_REBOOT
    bool "Reboot part-way through a soak run"
    default n
    depends on TK_DEBUG_SOAK
    select REBOOT
    help
      Deep sleep is a reboot, so a warm reboot is the closest thing to a wake
      that exists before CONFIG_PM does. Turning this on makes a soak run
      exercise the retained block over and over unattended: the shuffle bag,
      the refresh counter and the question on the glass either come back or
      they do not, and the boot log says which.

      Warm, not cold — a cold reset takes the RTC domain with it.

config TK_DEBUG_SOAK_REBOOT_EVERY
    int "Presses between reboots"
    default 3
    depends on TK_DEBUG_SOAK_REBOOT
    help
      Small enough that a run produces reboots quickly, large enough that the
      questions either side of one can be compared.

config TK_SLEEP
    bool "Enter deep sleep when the table goes quiet"
    default n
    depends on !TK_DEBUG_SOAK && !TK_DEBUG_CHARSET
    select POWEROFF
    select PM
    help
      Below 30 uA means deep sleep, and on the ESP32-S3 deep sleep is a reboot:
      SRAM and every thread stack are gone, and waking runs main() from the top.
      Everything that has to outlive that is already in RTC memory, so this is
      the switch that starts using it.

      Off by default here and turned on by the devkit board conf, so the
      ordinary image sleeps.

      Structurally unavailable in the two debug images, because a wake is a
      reboot and both keep state in ordinary memory across presses: a soak run
      counts its own presses, and the charset image remembers which test page it
      is on. Neither survives a wake, so rather than producing a confusing run,
      sleeping is not offered.

config TK_PANEL_DEEP_SLEEP
    bool "Put the panel controller to sleep too"
    default n
    depends on TK_SLEEP
    select SSD16XX_PRESERVE_IMAGE_HW_RESET
    help
      sys_poweroff() stops the SoC and nothing else. The SSD1680 is a separate
      chip on its own rail, and Zephyr's ssd16xx driver never issues command
      0x10 — SSD16XX_CMD_SLEEP_MODE is defined in ssd16xx_regs.h and used
      nowhere — so through every deep sleep the controller sits in whatever
      state the last refresh left it in. CONFIG_PM_DEVICE does not change
      that: sys_poweroff() runs no device hooks, and the driver defines no PM
      action for them to run.

      Turning this on sends the controller into its deep sleep mode 1 (RAM
      retained) on the way down, and takes the hardware reset pulse back at
      init so it can be revived — which is what the select above is for, since
      a sleeping controller ignores the bus.

      Off by default, and a bench question rather than a settled one. The
      saving has not been measured and neither has the cost: the reset that
      wakes the controller may take the RAM with it, in which case every wake
      falls back to a full refresh and this is not worth having. Measure both
      before shipping it. See docs/hardware_wiring.md, "Bench measurements".

config TK_SLEEP_IDLE_MS
    int "Quiet time before sleeping (ms)"
    default 2000
    depends on TK_SLEEP
    help
      Measured from the last press, deck change or refresh. A shipped device
      wants this short, since the panel holds its image and waking is a whole
      boot either way. Longer is easier to work with on a bench, where a device
      that sleeps between two console lines is hard to talk to.

config TK_REFRESH_TIMEOUT_MS
    int "How long to wait for the display before giving up (ms)"
    default 15000
    help
      Only there so a dead panel cannot wedge the device, so it should sit well
      clear of the slowest honest refresh — a full update on the GDEY0213B74 is
      seconds. Too short is much worse than too long: the state machine gives
      up, a late render then arrives for a question nobody is waiting for, and
      presses get dropped.

      Measured on the Waveshare 2.13-inch V4: a partial refresh takes 622 ms,
      steady to within 2 ms across 193 of them, and the full refresh at boot
      takes 2315 ms. The full one is the case this has to clear.

      15 s is therefore about six and a half times the slowest honest refresh,
      and it stays there rather than being tightened onto the measurement.
      E-paper slows down markedly in the cold and this rig has only been run at
      room temperature, so the margin covers a condition that has not been
      tested. Nothing is paid for it: the timeout is reached only when a panel
      has actually failed, and then it costs a few seconds once.

config TK_FULL_REFRESH_INTERVAL
    int "Partial refreshes between full refreshes"
    default 16
    help
      Set from the glass rather than from the soak run. A `just fw-soak` run on
      the Waveshare 2.13-inch V4 reached 193 consecutive partial refreshes with
      only minor artefacts, and 64 was picked as three times under that — but
      ghosting showed in ordinary use well before 64. The run's "minor
      artefacts" turned out to be a more forgiving standard than a question
      somebody is reading at a table. 16 is where the panel still looks clean;
      the 193 stands as a ceiling to stay under, not a target to approach.

      A cold boot always uses a full refresh because the panel content is
      unknown. A deep-sleep wake is also a fresh boot, but not an unknown
      panel: the controller keeps power and keeps its RAM, so the counter
      carries on across the wake from RTC memory rather than restarting. Both
      halves of that are load-bearing — see docs/firmware_architecture.md,
      "Where state lives", and CONFIG_SSD16XX_PRESERVE_IMAGE_ON_INIT in
      firmware/patches.yml.

config TK_PLAYBACK_DEPTH_MAX
    int "Maximum editorial depth in normal playback"
    default 2
    range 1 3
    help
      Depth 3 is never drawn automatically. Changing this away from 2 breaks
      the consent model in docs/design.md.

config TK_RECENT_RING
    int "Questions excluded from the start of a new bag cycle"
    default 20
    help
      Shared across all decks, so turning the selector cannot immediately
      return a question just seen under a different deck.

      The smallest shipped deck is the same size as this ring (Here has 20
      eligible questions in English and 12 in German), so the ring can exclude
      an entire deck. The bag relaxes it rather than failing; see lib/qdb.

config TK_MAX_QUESTIONS
    int "Largest corpus the bag can track"
    default 512
    help
      Sets the width of the per-deck drawn bitmap kept in RTC memory: one bit
      per question per deck, so 512 costs 384 bytes of the 8 KB available.
      English ships 240. A bundle with more questions than this is rejected at
      open() rather than silently half-tracked.

config TK_DEBUG_CORPUS_STORE
    bool "Write the compiled-in corpus to the filesystem at boot"
    default n
    depends on FILE_SYSTEM_LITTLEFS
    help
      Stores the compiled-in corpus for the chosen language under /corpus at
      boot, so the *next* boot opens it from the filesystem instead.

      It exists because the store, the atomic rename and the read-back are the
      whole of what sync does with a corpus once it has verified one, and none
      of it needs a radio to be wrong. Without this there is no way to exercise
      any of it until the download path exists, and then a failure could be in
      either half.

      The boot after this says `stored corpus <lang>` rather than falling back,
      which is the difference to watch for. Never enabled in a shipped build.

config TK_MAX_CORPUS_BYTES
    int "Largest synced corpus the device will read (bytes)"
    default 32768
    help
      A corpus that arrives over the network is copied into RAM, because qdb
      hands out pointers into the bundle and a file's bytes are not contiguous
      in flash.

      Sized against TK_MAX_QUESTIONS rather than against what ships today: 512
      questions is the most qdb will accept, and English is 15 KB at 240. A
      bundle larger than this is refused before it is written, since a corpus
      the device cannot read back would be worse stored than not stored — it
      would have replaced one that works.

      Costs this many bytes of RAM whenever a filesystem is configured.

config TK_MAX_QUESTION_BYTES
    int "Largest question the device will render (UTF-8 bytes)"
    default 128
    help
      Bounds the copy that travels on chan_question. The longest shipped
      question is 94 bytes (German); the qdb suite asserts the real bundles
      fit, so a corpus change that breaks this fails a test rather than a
      device.

config TK_NET
    bool "Wi-Fi, and the setup portal that configures it"
    default n
    help
      Brings in the Wi-Fi driver, the networking stack, the HTTP server and
      NVS. The device joins a stored network at boot, and holding both buttons
      through a boot raises a SoftAP with a captive portal on it.

      Off by default, for the reason TK_SLEEP is: this is a large subsystem
      whose flash and RAM cost has not been measured on this board, and the
      everyday `just fw-build` image should not change under it. Use
      `just fw-net`, which merges app/net.conf and app/net.overlay.

      The device works with its compiled-in corpus whether or not this is on.
      Wi-Fi is optional by design; see docs/design.md.

config TK_DEBUG_PORTAL
    bool "Enter setup at every boot, without the gesture"
    default n
    depends on TK_NET
    help
      Skips the both-buttons hold and starts the portal straight from boot.

      The gesture needs two hands on the board at the moment it starts, which
      makes every other part of the portal awkward to work on: raising the
      access point, the DNS answers, the pages and the join all have to be
      reached through it before they can be looked at.

      Never enabled in a shipped build, and it says so in the boot log.

config TK_SYNC
    bool "Fetch question bundles over the network"
    default n
    depends on TK_NET
    help
      Checks a manifest, downloads a newer bundle, verifies it, and replaces
      the stored corpus. The full flow is docs/sync_protocol.md.

      Off unless the host below is a real one. A device that cannot reach its
      manifest retries forever for nothing, and the placeholder domain is not
      reachable — see docs/decisions.md.

config TK_SYNC_BASE_URL
    string "Where the manifest and bundles are served from"
    default "http://tischkarte.invalid/device"
    depends on TK_SYNC
    help
      Everything under it: `<base>/manifest.json`, and the bundle URLs the
      manifest names. An object store, a small static host, or a laptop during
      development — anything that serves two files.

      `http`, and the host must not upgrade it. A redirect to HTTPS is fatal
      rather than slow: Zephyr's HTTP client does not follow redirects, so the
      device sees a 301 it cannot act on. That rules out Cloudflare Pages and
      GitHub Pages, and it is why this is not the same host as the website.
      GitHub Releases is out for the same reason.

      The default is deliberately unresolvable. A device pointed at a host that
      does not exist retries a handful of seconds per cold boot and carries on;
      one pointed at a host somebody else owns is a different matter.

config TK_SYNC_HOST
    string "Host name to connect to and resolve"
    default "tischkarte.invalid"
    depends on TK_SYNC
    help
      The host part of TK_SYNC_BASE_URL, separately, because the socket needs
      it before there is a URL to parse and TLS wants it for SNI.

config TK_SYNC_PORT
    int "Port"
    default 80
    depends on TK_SYNC

config TK_SYNC_INSECURE
    bool "Connect without TLS"
    default y
    depends on TK_SYNC
    help
      Plain HTTP, and this is the shipped configuration rather than a
      development shortcut. See the decision log.

      The device has no clock, so it cannot validate a certificate under any
      scheme — TLS here would authenticate nobody and buy only confidentiality,
      on artifacts that are public in a public repository. What protects a
      bundle and an image is the Ed25519 signature over each, verified against
      keys that do not expire.

      What it gives up: an observer learns that a Tischkarte fetched something.
      What it does not give up: an attacker on the path still cannot make the
      device install anything the keys did not sign, and cannot walk it
      backwards, because a manifest older than what is installed is refused.

config TK_OTA
    bool "Install firmware updates over the network"
    default n
    depends on TK_SYNC
    depends on BOOTLOADER_MCUBOOT
    select STREAM_FLASH
    select IMG_MANAGER
    select IMG_ERASE_PROGRESSIVELY
    select REBOOT
    help
      Checks a firmware manifest, downloads a newer image into the spare
      MCUboot slot, verifies it, and marks it for install on the next boot.
      The full flow is docs/firmware_update.md.

      Depends on TK_SYNC rather than standing alone: both speak to the same
      host over the same socket code, and the signature this verifies is made
      with the same key as a question bundle's.

      Depends on BOOTLOADER_MCUBOOT because without a bootloader there is no
      second slot to write to — which means this stays off in a build without
      `--sysbuild`, and such a build produces a working device that cannot
      update itself. `just fw-build` passes --sysbuild.

      STREAM_FLASH is selected explicitly because IMG_MANAGER depends on it
      rather than selecting it, and a `select` skips dependencies: without
      this line the build fails inside Zephyr's own Kconfig with a warning
      about STREAM_FLASH_ERASE.

      MCUBOOT_IMG_MANAGER is not selected here, and cannot be: it is a choice
      symbol, and Kconfig ignores select on those. It is the only member of
      that choice and its default, so enabling IMG_MANAGER is what turns it
      on.

      IMG_ERASE_PROGRESSIVELY erases as the download arrives rather than
      erasing 1344 KB up front. The device is awake and on the radio either
      way; this spreads the cost instead of stalling before the first byte.

      REBOOT because installing is a restart: MCUboot does the copy, and the
      only way to reach it is to go through it.

config TK_OTA_BASE_URL
    string "Where the firmware manifest and images are served from"
    default "http://tischkarte.invalid/device"
    depends on TK_OTA
    help
      `<base>/firmware.json`, and the image URL that manifest names.

      Separate from TK_SYNC_BASE_URL so a development image can take firmware
      from a laptop while still taking questions from the site, or the reverse.
      Both still resolve TK_SYNC_HOST — one host per image, because the socket
      needs a name before there is a URL to parse.

config TK_OTA_ON_COLD_BOOT
    bool "Check for firmware on a cold boot, after the corpus sync"
    default y
    depends on TK_OTA
    help
      The same trigger the corpus sync uses, and for the same reasons: a cold
      boot is rare once the device sleeps, nobody is waiting on it, and the
      device is most likely to be plugged in. Turning this off leaves the
      portal's sync button as the only way to update.

config TK_PORTAL_SSID_PREFIX
    string "Setup network name, before the per-device suffix"
    default "Tischkarte"
    depends on TK_NET
    help
      The SoftAP announces itself as `<prefix>-XXXX`, where the suffix comes
      from the chip's device id. Two devices being set up on the same table
      would otherwise raise two networks with the same name.

config TK_PORTAL_ENTRY_HOLD_MS
    int "Both buttons held this long after boot to enter setup (ms)"
    default 2000
    depends on TK_NET
    help
      The documented gesture is USB-plus-Next, which needs VBUS detect on
      GPIO21 — reserved, but not wired and not in the devicetree. Both buttons
      at boot is what the current hardware can sense.

      The hold is confirmed rather than sampled once, because somebody picking
      the device up with two fingers should not find themselves in a service
      mode. Two seconds is long enough to be deliberate and short enough that
      nobody wonders whether it worked.

config TK_PORTAL_WINDOW_MS
    int "How long the portal stays up (ms)"
    default 300000
    help
      A hard window rather than an idle timer, and measured from the moment the
      portal starts serving. An open access point that any passing phone can
      keep alive by probing it is a worse thing to leave on a table than one
      that closes on its own five minutes later.

      The window restarts once, when a network is joined, so the status page
      can be read afterwards. Re-entering setup is a reboot with both buttons.

config TK_PORTAL_SCAN_MS
    int "How long to wait for the scan before giving up on it (ms)"
    default 8000
    help
      The scan runs before the access point, because the ESP32-S3 has one
      radio: scanning with a phone already associated stalls it and can drop
      the association.

      Expiring is survivable. The setup page takes a typed network name — which
      a hidden network needs anyway — so a missing list costs some typing
      rather than the whole flow.

config TK_NET_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS
    int "How long to wait for a network to accept us (ms)"
    default 20000
    help
      Covers association, authentication and DHCP. Expiring puts the portal
      back on the setup page with the panel saying so, because the likely cause
      is a mistyped password and the fix is to type it again.

config TK_NET_SCAN_MAX
    int "Networks kept from a scan"
    default 12
    depends on TK_NET
    help
      Bounds the array the setup page renders from. A busy 2.4 GHz band has
      more than this, and the rest are dropped: a list nobody can scroll is not
      more useful than a short one, and each entry costs 34 bytes of RAM that
      is live for the whole portal session.

config TK_POWER
    bool "Measure the cell, and decide what its voltage means"
    default n
    select ADC
    help
      Samples the battery through a divider on ADC1 and VBUS through a divider
      on a plain input, turns the pair into a state with lib/power, and
      publishes it on chan_power. That state gates panel refreshes, holds the
      device awake while it is charging, and drives the status LEDs.

      Off by default here and turned on by the devkit board conf, for the
      reason TK_SLEEP is: the everyday `just fw-build` image should not change
      under a board that has no divider fitted. Without this the device works
      exactly as it did — it simply never refuses a refresh and never knows it
      is plugged in.

      Wiring, and the reason each pin is that pin, is docs/hardware_wiring.md.

config TK_REFRESH_MIN_MV
    int "Minimum cell voltage for a panel refresh (mV)"
    default 3200
    help
      A partial refresh attempted near brownout can leave the panel in a
      corrupted state. Below this, the previous question stays on screen.

      Also where LOW begins, so this is the number that decides when the device
      stops answering presses. Read by lib/power; before the power module
      existed it was declared here and nothing looked at it.

      Not yet measured. The bench number to find is where a refresh actually
      corrupts, which is not where the regulator gives up: the 3.3 V buck on
      the charger board passes the cell straight through below about 3.45 V, so
      the rail sags with the cell rather than cutting off, and the panel keeps
      working for a while after that.

config TK_POWER_CRITICAL_MV
    int "Cell voltage below which the device says so loudly (mV)"
    default 3000
    depends on TK_POWER
    help
      Both LOW and CRITICAL refuse a refresh; this is where the answer changes
      from one amber blink to three red ones. Set at the knee of a lithium
      discharge curve, where the remaining capacity is small and falling fast.

config TK_POWER_PLAUSIBLE_MV
    int "Lowest pack reading that could be a cell (mV)"
    default 2500
    depends on TK_POWER
    help
      Under this, the reading is not a cell and the machine says UNKNOWN — which
      permits refreshes — rather than walking down to CRITICAL and refusing
      them.

      A protected 18650 disconnects between 2.5 and 3.0 V, and the 3.3 V buck
      stops before that, so an SoC still running cannot be reading 2.4 V from
      its own pack. What it is reading is a divider that is not there: a
      floating ADC pin, a broken solder joint, an unpopulated rig. All of those
      arrive as some low number and none of them is a reason to stop drawing
      questions.

      2500 rather than something nearer zero because the failure to catch is a
      floating pin picking up a volt or so of anything, not a pin reading
      exactly nought.

config TK_POWER_FULL_MV
    int "Cell voltage that counts as charged (mV)"
    default 4050
    depends on TK_POWER
    help
      An estimate, and it must not be read as anything else. The bq25185 on the
      bench board exposes no charge-status pin, so termination cannot be
      sensed; a cell under constant-voltage charge sits near 4.2 V for the last
      hour, so this reports full early by an amount that depends on the load.

      Acceptable for an LED somebody glances at. Not acceptable as an input to
      anything that decides something, which is why nothing else reads it.

      Keep it well under the charger's own regulation voltage. Two things eat
      the gap from opposite ends: the divider reads about 40 mV low on the
      bench, and constant-voltage charge approaches 4.2 V asymptotically while
      the system draws its share of the current, so the last tens of millivolts
      can take an hour or never arrive. A threshold set close to 4.2 V is one
      the LED never reaches, which costs the whole green half of the palette to
      buy precision in an estimate that is not precise.

      4050 here is roughly 4090 mV at the cell, which is a full-enough LiPo for
      something somebody glances at across a table.

config TK_POWER_HYSTERESIS_MV
    int "Millivolts a reading must clear a threshold by to cross back (mV)"
    default 120
    depends on TK_POWER
    help
      Applied on the way up only. Getting worse is reported immediately,
      because being slow in that direction costs something.

      120 mV because a partial refresh sags the pack by more than that for two
      seconds, so a cell that reads under a threshold during one is not
      necessarily under it. The sampler skips readings taken during a refresh
      for the same reason; this covers what the skip does not.

config TK_POWER_SAMPLE_MS
    int "Time between readings while awake (ms)"
    default 10000
    depends on TK_POWER
    help
      Rarely reached on battery, where the device is awake for about
      TK_SLEEP_IDLE_MS per press and the reading that matters is the one taken
      at boot. This is for the two windows that last minutes or hours: a charge,
      and a portal session.

config TK_POWER_CONFIRM_SAMPLES
    int "Readings averaged into the one taken at boot"
    default 4
    range 1 16
    depends on TK_POWER
    help
      The boot reading is the only one a battery-powered press will ever get,
      so it is a burst averaged rather than a single conversion. Four is enough
      to flatten ADC noise and costs well under a millisecond.

      This is where all the smoothing lives. A "wait for N consecutive
      readings" rule inside the state machine would be dead code on battery:
      the machine is constructed, sees one or two samples, and is destroyed by
      sys_poweroff() a couple of seconds later.

config TK_POWER_CHARGE_WINDOW_MS
    int "How long a plug-in keeps the sync and update window open (ms)"
    default 300000
    depends on TK_POWER
    help
      Bounds the window, not wakefulness. The device stays awake for as long as
      external power is present — the status LED needs it running — but a
      device left on the charger overnight should not retry a sync for eight
      hours.

      One window per plug-in. Reaching a full cell part-way through does not
      buy a second one.

config TK_POWER_DIVIDER_NUM
    int "Battery divider, numerator"
    default 2
    depends on TK_POWER
    help
      Millivolts at the pack are the reading times NUM over DEN. The bench
      divider is 470k over 470k, so the pack is twice the tap: 2 over 1.

      Two integers rather than one ratio because a divider made of real
      resistors is rarely a round number, and calibrating against a multimeter
      is the last step of bringing the rig up.

config TK_POWER_DIVIDER_DEN
    int "Battery divider, denominator"
    default 1
    depends on TK_POWER

config TK_POWER_WAKE_ON_USB
    bool "Wake from deep sleep when USB is plugged in"
    default n
    depends on TK_POWER
    help
      Off, and the default is the recommendation rather than an oversight.

      The buttons wake through EXT1, which takes one trigger polarity for the
      whole pin mask, and VBUS is high when it is interesting — so VBUS cannot
      join that mask. It uses EXT0 instead, a separate single-pin trigger with
      its own polarity, armed by src/sleep.c from the same tk-vbus-gpios
      property src/power.c samples.

      What turning this on costs is in Espressif's sleep_modes.c: arming EXT0
      forces ESP_PD_DOMAIN_RTC_PERIPH to stay powered through every sleep, where
      EXT1 alone leaves it off. That is a permanent standby cost against a 30 uA
      budget, paid on every sleep for the life of the device, to save one button
      press — plugging in and then pressing Next is the gesture the design
      specified anyway.

      How much it costs has not been measured, which is the reason the symbol
      exists: turn it on, sleep the board, and put a meter on it.

      An EXT0 wake reports no button, so the device replays no press and `net`
      takes its cold-boot branch — plugging in wakes the device and syncs it.

config TK_DEBUG_POWER
    bool "Log the cell voltage continuously"
    default n
    depends on TK_POWER
    help
      Samples every second regardless of TK_POWER_SAMPLE_MS and logs the raw
      conversion beside the converted millivolts, so a discharge can be
      recorded unattended and the divider constants calibrated against a
      multimeter.

      Set by `just fw-power`. Never enabled in a shipped build.

config TK_STATUS_LED
    bool "Show power, the portal and transfers on two LEDs"
    default n
    depends on TK_POWER
    help
      A red LED and a green one, driven as plain GPIOs. Lighting both is amber,
      which is the whole palette: the conditions that share a colour are told
      apart by rhythm instead.

      No driver to select. An addressable LED was rejected on current — the
      controller inside a WS2812 idles at around 0.6 mA with the emitters dark,
      which is twenty times the whole sleep budget, where two GPIOs held low
      cost nothing at all.

      Compiled out on a board with no tk_leds devicetree node, which is qemu,
      native_sim, and any board that simply has no LEDs.

config TK_STATUS_LED_BLINK_MS
    int "On, and off, time of one blink (ms)"
    default 200
    depends on TK_STATUS_LED
    help
      Fast enough that three of them read as one deliberate answer to a press
      rather than as three separate events.

config TK_STATUS_LED_PULSE_MS
    int "Full cycle of the transfer pulse (ms)"
    default 1500
    depends on TK_STATUS_LED
    help
      Slow, so it does not compete with a blink for attention. Long enough to
      read as "something is running" rather than as a fault.
